// Sections 138-141: targets and build.

render_section_138 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\One compiler binary emits code for every target it supports, and
        \\`--target` selects the target for a build. With no `--target` the
        \\build is for the host, the machine the compiler process is running on.
        \\The choice is settled before checking begins, because the constant
        \\`OUT` describes the target and gated code reads it while types are
        \\being resolved.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\A target is three named fields: `os`, `cpu` and `abi`. The LLVM triple
        \\handed to the backend is derived from those fields and is never
        \\written directly. The derivation loses information:
        \\`x86_64-freestanding-uefi` and `x86_64-windows-msvc` both produce the
        \\triple `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, and only the `abi` field separates
        \\the two builds.
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --target x86_64-linux-musl -o hello hello.glo
        \\glosso: compile hello.glo (target=x86_64-pc-linux-musl)
        \\glosso: link hello (target=x86_64-pc-linux-musl, -O1)
        \\glosso: done hello  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-pc-linux-musl, -O1)
    );
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\Every progress line names the resolved triple, so the first line of a
        \\build is the confirmation that the spelling was understood. That
        \\Linux executable was produced on a Windows host with nothing else
        \\installed: the musl runtime for the target is bundled with the
        \\compiler, and the result is a static ELF carrying no dynamic
        \\interpreter.
    );
    subheading(4, "Spelling a target");
    paragraph(5, #string,oneline
        \\`--target` accepts both `os-cpu-abi` and `cpu-os-abi`. The parser
        \\decides which order was written by testing whether the first field is
        \\a known CPU name and the second a known operating-system name; there
        \\is no table of complete triples to match against. Any field left out
        \\is inherited from the host target.
    );
    bullet(6, #string,oneline
        \\A spec whose first field names a wasm CPU is read wasm first: `os`
        \\becomes `wasm`, `cpu` is the wasm word, and `abi` is the following
        \\field, defaulting to `freestanding`.
    );
    bullet(7, #string,oneline
        \\`<cpu>-freestanding` sets `os` and `abi` both to `freestanding`, so
        \\the CPU name in the first field is not mistaken for an operating
        \\system.
    );
    bullet(8, #string,oneline
        \\`wasi-wasm32` is accepted for compatibility and normalizes to `os`
        \\`wasm` with `abi` `wasi`; WASI is never exposed as an operating
        \\system. `host` and `native` name the compiler's own target. An empty
        \\spec is tolerated, and so is a trailing hyphen, so `wasm32-` resolves
        \\to `wasm32-unknown-unknown`.
    );
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\Field names are rewritten to a canonical spelling before the triple is
        \\formed. For the CPU field, `amd64` becomes `x86_64`, `arm64` becomes
        \\`aarch64`, `i686` becomes `i386`, and `ppc` and `ppc32` become
        \\`powerpc`. For the operating-system field, `darwin` and `osx` become
        \\`macos`, `win32` becomes `windows`, and `webassembly` and `wasi`
        \\become `wasm`. Three further rewrites happen only in the emitted
        \\triple: `x86` is spelled `i686`, `armv5` is spelled `armv5te`, and
        \\`ppc64le` is spelled `powerpc64le`.
    );
    note(10, #string,oneline
        \\A spec with a single field fills the operating-system slot, not the
        \\CPU slot. `--target aarch64` therefore resolves to
        \\`x86_64-unknown-aarch64-msvc` and dies at the link step with
        \\`error: unsupported executable target` naming that triple. For the
        \\same reason `--target x86_64-windows` leaves `abi` empty, which
        \\triples as `x86_64-w64-windows-gnu` rather than the MSVC ABI of a
        \\Windows host. Write the full three-field spelling.
    );
    subheading(11, "The supported set");
    paragraph(12, #string,oneline
        \\`--print-targets` prints each supported target with the triple it
        \\expands to and the artifact it produces, then a second list headed
        \\`Available LLVM codegen targets:`. That second list is the set of
        \\architectures the linked LLVM was built with; it is not the set of
        \\Glosso targets, and naming one of its entries does not make a target.
    );
    table_header(13, "Target   —   LLVM triple   —   Artifact");
    table_row(14, "`host`, `native`   |   the compiler's own triple   |   whatever that target produces");
    table_row(15, "`x86_64-windows-msvc`   |   `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`   |   PE/COFF executable");
    table_row(16, "`x86_64-windows-gnu`   |   `x86_64-w64-windows-gnu`   |   PE/COFF executable");
    table_row(17, "`linux-x86_64-gnu`   |   `x86_64-pc-linux-gnu`   |   ELF executable");
    table_row(18, "`aarch64-linux-gnu`   |   `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`   |   ELF executable");
    table_row(19, "`x86_64-macos`, `aarch64-macos`   |   `x86_64-apple-darwin`, `aarch64-apple-darwin`   |   Mach-O executable");
    table_row(20, "`x86_64-freebsd`, `aarch64-freebsd`   |   `x86_64-unknown-freebsd`, `aarch64-unknown-freebsd`   |   ELF executable");
    table_row(21, "`wasm32-wasi`, `wasm64-wasi`   |   `wasm32-unknown-wasi`, `wasm64-unknown-wasi`   |   WebAssembly module");
    table_row(22, "`wasm32-freestanding`   |   `wasm32-unknown-unknown`   |   WebAssembly module");
    table_row(23, "`x86_64-freestanding`   |   `x86_64-unknown-none-elf`   |   bare-metal ELF executable");
    table_row(24, "`x86_64-freestanding-uefi`   |   `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`   |   UEFI PE/COFF object");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\Fourteen further targets share the shape `<cpu>-linux-musl` and link
        \\against the bundled static musl runtime: `x86`, `x86_64`, `aarch64`,
        \\`armv5`, `armv7`, `loongarch64`, `mips`, `mipsel`, `mips64`,
        \\`mips64el`, `powerpc`, `ppc64le`, `riscv64` and `s390x`. Each expands
        \\to `<cpu>-unknown-linux-musl` with five exceptions:
        \\`linux-x86_64-musl` is `x86_64-pc-linux-musl`, `x86-linux-musl` is
        \\`i686-unknown-linux-musl`, `ppc64le-linux-musl` is
        \\`powerpc64le-unknown-linux-musl`, `armv5-linux-musl` is
        \\`armv5te-unknown-linux-musleabi`, and `armv7-linux-musl` is
        \\`armv7-unknown-linux-musleabihf`.
    );
    subheading(26, "Entry points");
    paragraph(27, #string,oneline
        \\A Glosso `main` is lowered under the symbol `glo_main`. What calls it
        \\depends on the target family: a hosted target gets a synthesized C
        \\entry point that forwards to `glo_main`, and a target with no host
        \\runtime has `glo_main` named as the entry or exported directly.
    );
    table_header(28, "Target family   —   Emitted entry   —   Relation to `glo_main`");
    table_row(29, "hosted Windows   |   `wmain(i32, ptr)`   |   wrapper that calls it");
    table_row(30, "hosted ELF and Mach-O   |   `main(i32, ptr)`   |   wrapper that calls it");
    table_row(31, "`wasm32-wasi`, `wasm64-wasi`   |   `_start()`   |   wrapper that calls it, linked with `--export=_start`");
    table_row(32, "`wasm32-freestanding`   |   none   |   linked with `--export=glo_main`");
    table_row(33, "`<cpu>-freestanding`   |   none   |   linked with `-e glo_main`, no argv");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\`--no-main` and the `static-lib` and `shared-lib` backends suppress
        \\the wrapper entirely, along with the `argc` and `argv` globals, and
        \\root reachability at every defined `#c_call` function instead. Being
        \\Called from C describes that rooting, and Backends, Artifacts, and
        \\Optimization Levels describes the backends.
    );
    subheading(35, "What links from a given host");
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\Emitting an object succeeds for every supported target, because that
        \\step needs only LLVM. Linking needs the target's runtime inputs. The
        \\compiler bundles the musl runtime, the mingw-w64 import libraries and
        \\the wasm builtins, so those targets link from any host; Apple and
        \\FreeBSD system libraries are not bundled and must come from an SDK.
    );
    bullet(37, #string,oneline
        \\Measured on an `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` host, these link with no
        \\further setup: both Windows targets, `linux-x86_64-gnu`, all fourteen
        \\musl targets, both WASI targets, `wasm32-freestanding` and
        \\`x86_64-freestanding`.
    );
    bullet(38, #string,oneline
        \\These reach the linker and fail there: `x86_64-macos` and
        \\`aarch64-macos` on `ld64.lld: error: undefined symbol: _abort`, the
        \\two FreeBSD targets on `ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: abort`, and
        \\`aarch64-linux-gnu` on `ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: write`.
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --target aarch64-macos -o hello hello.glo
        \\glosso: compile hello.glo (target=aarch64-apple-darwin)
        \\glosso: link hello (target=aarch64-apple-darwin, -O1)
        \\error: ld64.lld: error: undefined symbol: _abort
        \\>>> referenced by C:\Users\…\Temp\glosso-35100-hello.o:(symbol _glo_write_string+0x248)
        \\
        \\$ glosso --backend object --target aarch64-macos -o hello.o hello.glo
        \\glosso: compile hello.glo (target=aarch64-apple-darwin)
        \\glosso: wrote hello.o  (backend=object, target=aarch64-apple-darwin, -O1)
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\`--backend object` is therefore the portable smoke test: it proves
        \\that a program checks and lowers for a target without requiring that
        \\target's linker inputs. `x86_64-freestanding-uefi` produces an object
        \\by design, and Freestanding, Bare Metal, UEFI, and WebAssembly covers
        \\the link step it needs.
    );
    subheading(41, "The OUT and HOST descriptors");
    paragraph(42, #string,oneline
        \\The checker installs two constants into every compilation. `OUT` is an
        \\`Output_Target` with the fields `os`, `cpu` and `abi`, describing the
        \\target being compiled for. `HOST` is a `Host_Target` with `os` and
        \\`cpu`, describing the machine running the compiler. Their field types
        \\come from the Prelude and are always in scope, so reading them needs
        \\no import.
    );
    table_header(43, "Type   —   Members");
    table_row(44, "`Target_OS`   |   `Unknown`, `Linux`, `Macos`, `Windows`, `Freebsd`, `Wasm`, `Freestanding`");
    table_row(45, "`Target_CPU`   |   `Unknown`, `X86_64`, `Aarch64`, `Arm`, `Thumb`, `I386`, `Powerpc`, `Powerpc64`, `Powerpc64le`, `Riscv64`, `Wasm32`, `Wasm64`, `Armv7`, `Loongarch64`, `S390x`, `X86`, `Armv5`, `Mips`, `Mipsel`, `Mips64`, `Mips64el`");
    table_row(46, "`Target_ABI`   |   `None`, `Unknown`, `Gnu`, `Musl`, `Msvc`, `Mingw`, `Wasi`, `Freestanding`, `Emscripten`, `Uefi`");
    table_row(47, "`Target_Endian`   |   `Little`, `Big`, returned by `target_endian()`");
    code_block(48, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\os_name :: () -> string {
        \\    #if OUT.os == {
        \\    case .Windows;      return "windows";
        \\    case .Linux;        return "linux";
        \\    case .Macos;        return "macos";
        \\    case .Freebsd;      return "freebsd";
        \\    case .Wasm;         return "wasm";
        \\    case .Freestanding; return "freestanding";
        \\    else;               return "unknown";
        \\    }
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("building for %\n", os_name());
        \\    print("OUT.abi is Msvc: %\n", OUT.abi == Target_ABI.Msvc);
        \\    print("host is the target: %\n", OUT.os == HOST.os && OUT.cpu == HOST.cpu);
        \\}
    );
    code_block(49, "text", #string
        \\building for windows
        \\OUT.abi is Msvc: 1
        \\host is the target: 1
    );
    paragraph(50, #string,oneline
        \\`%` prints an enum member as its integer value, which is why the
        \\example maps the tag to a string rather than printing `OUT.os`
        \\directly. Both the bare `OUT.os == .Windows` and the qualified
        \\`OUT.os == Target_OS.Windows` are accepted.
    );
    paragraph(51, #string,oneline
        \\`#if` over `OUT` selects target-specific code, and `#compile_error`
        \\rejects a target the program does not support. Static Branching with
        \\`#if` covers the construct itself. Both directives are statements, so
        \\a gate lives inside a declaration rather than at file scope.
    );
    code_block(52, "glosso", #string
        \\path_separator :: () -> string {
        \\    #if OUT.cpu != Target_CPU.X86_64 && OUT.cpu != Target_CPU.Aarch64
        \\        #compile_error "this program supports x86_64 and aarch64 only";
        \\    #if OUT.os == {
        \\    case .Windows;  return "\\";
        \\    else;           return "/";
        \\    }
        \\}
    );
    code_block(53, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --target riscv64-linux-musl gating.glo
        \\glosso: compile gating.glo (target=riscv64-unknown-linux-musl)
        \\error: this program supports x86_64 and aarch64 only
        \\  --> …\gating.glo:5:9
        \\  |
        \\5 |         #compile_error "this program supports x86_64 and aarch64 only";
        \\  |         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    note(54, #string,oneline
        \\The LLVM triple is not a unique target key. `x86_64-freestanding-uefi`
        \\and `x86_64-windows-msvc` share `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, so code that
        \\must tell them apart tests `OUT.abi`. The artifact cache keys on the
        \\`OUT` fields for exactly this reason; The Build Cache and Generated
        \\Sources shows the two builds keeping separate entries.
    );
    paragraph(55, #string,oneline
        \\`--run` builds and then executes the result, and is refused for
        \\anything but the host: `glosso: --run can only execute the host target
        \\(got x86_64-pc-linux-musl)`, exit status 1. Using the Compiler covers
        \\the remaining driver options.
    );
}

render_section_139 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Three targets run with no hosted operating system underneath them:
        \\`x86_64-freestanding`, `x86_64-freestanding-uefi` and
        \\`wasm32-freestanding`. What they share is that the backend emits no
        \\host entry wrapper and no command-line globals, and that nothing links
        \\a C runtime in behind the program's back. The WASI targets are the
        \\hosted wasm option and are covered here for contrast.
    );
    paragraph(1, #string,oneline
        \\The predicate the backend uses for bare metal is `os == "freestanding"`.
        \\That is true for `x86_64-freestanding` and for the UEFI target, and
        \\false for the wasm targets, whose `os` is `wasm`. The families
        \\therefore differ in the details below even though they share the same
        \\absence of a host.
    );
    subheading(2, "Bare-metal ELF");
    paragraph(3, #string,oneline
        \\`x86_64-freestanding` expands to `x86_64-unknown-none-elf` and sets
        \\both `OUT.os` and `OUT.abi` to `Freestanding`. The image is linked with
        \\`ld.lld -e glo_main`, so the Glosso `main` is the entry point and there
        \\is no `argc`/`argv` pair to receive. When the program has no `main` at
        \\all, reachability is rooted at every defined `#c_call` function, the
        \\same rule `--no-main` installs.
    );
    code_block(4, "glosso", #string
        \\kernel_add :: (a: s32, b: s32) -> s32 #c_call { return a + b; }
        \\
        \\_start :: () #c_call {
        \\    x := kernel_add(2, 3);
        \\    while true {}
        \\}
    );
    code_block(5, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --target x86_64-freestanding -o bare.elf bare.glo
        \\glosso: compile bare.glo (target=x86_64-unknown-none-elf)
        \\glosso: link bare.elf (target=x86_64-unknown-none-elf, -O2)
        \\glosso: done bare.elf  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-unknown-none-elf, -O2)
    );
    paragraph(6, #string,oneline
        \\The `-O2` is not decoration. At `-O0` and `-O1` the backend emits
        \\runtime safety checks, and a failing check terminates through `abort`,
        \\which no freestanding image provides. The default level is `-O1`, so
        \\the same command without an explicit level fails at the link step.
    );
    code_block(7, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --target x86_64-freestanding -o bare.elf bare.glo
        \\glosso: compile bare.glo (target=x86_64-unknown-none-elf)
        \\glosso: link bare.elf (target=x86_64-unknown-none-elf, -O1)
        \\error: ld.lld: error: undefined symbol: abort
        \\>>> referenced by bare
        \\>>>               C:\Users\…\Temp\glosso-33852-bare.o:(glo_kernel_add)
    );
    note(8, #string,oneline
        \\The rule that governs this is that runtime checks are emitted when the
        \\optimization level is 1 or less. The three size modes raise the
        \\internal level to 2 or 3, so `-Os`, `-Oz` and `-Ozz` also produce a
        \\linkable bare-metal image, and `-Ozz` is the usual choice. Overflow,
        \\Traps, and the `-O` Level covers the checks themselves.
    );
    bullet(9, #string,oneline
        \\No host entry is emitted: the module defines `glo_main` and no `main`
        \\or `wmain` wrapper, and the `__glo_argc` and `__glo_argv` globals are
        \\absent.
    );
    bullet(10, #string,oneline
        \\The default temporary-arena size drops from 4 MiB to 64 KiB on a
        \\freestanding target, because the arena is a zero-initialized global in
        \\the image. `--temp-allocator-size` overrides it in either direction.
    );
    bullet(11, #string,oneline
        \\Sanitizers are rejected by the backend before the link is attempted:
        \\`error: LLVM memory sanitizer is not supported for target
        \\'x86_64-unknown-none-elf'`.
    );
    subheading(12, "UEFI");
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\`x86_64-freestanding-uefi` keeps `OUT.os` at `Freestanding`, so no
        \\hosted runtime is injected, but maps to the LLVM triple
        \\`x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`, which selects PE/COFF and the Microsoft x64
        \\C ABI. `OUT.abi` is `Uefi`, and that field is the only way a program
        \\can tell a UEFI build from an ordinary Windows one.
        \\`x86_64-freestanding-msvc` is the legacy spelling and behaves
        \\identically.
    );
    paragraph(14, #string,oneline
        \\The executable backend cannot finish this target, because the COFF
        \\executable plan asks for the Microsoft C startup object and its
        \\`wmain`, which a firmware image does not have. The workflow is to emit
        \\an object and link it as an EFI application separately.
    );
    code_block(15, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --target x86_64-freestanding-uefi -o efi.exe efi.glo
        \\glosso: compile efi.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: link efi.exe (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O2)
        \\error: rust-lld: error: undefined symbol: wmain
    );
    code_block(16, "glosso", #string
        \\efi_main :: (image: *void, system_table: *void) -> u64 #c_call {
        \\    #if OUT.abi != Target_ABI.Uefi
        \\        #compile_error "efi.glo builds only for the UEFI ABI";
        \\    return 0;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(17, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --backend object \
        \\      --target x86_64-freestanding-uefi -o efi.obj efi.glo
        \\glosso: compile efi.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: wrote efi.obj  (backend=object, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O2)
        \\
        \\$ lld-link /nologo /machine:x64 /subsystem:efi_application \
        \\      /entry:glo_efi_main /nodefaultlib /opt:ref /out:efi.efi efi.obj
    );
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\The entry is spelled `glo_efi_main` because a `#c_call` definition is
        \\exported under `glo_` followed by its Glosso name; Being Called from C
        \\gives the full symbol rule. `/nodefaultlib` keeps the Microsoft CRT
        \\out, and `--no-main` on the Glosso side keeps the `main` wrapper out
        \\and roots reachability at the `#c_call` definitions.
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\Driving both steps from a build script is the usual arrangement, since
        \\the second step is an external command. The compile-time options
        \\mirror the flags: backend `Object`, target `os = .Freestanding`,
        \\`cpu = .X86_64`, `abi = .Uefi`, and `no_main = true`. Build Scripts
        \\covers the API.
    );
    subheading(20, "WebAssembly");
    table_header(21, "Target   —   Triple   —   Link");
    table_row(22, "`wasm32-wasi`   |   `wasm32-unknown-wasi`   |   `wasm-ld --no-entry --export=_start`");
    table_row(23, "`wasm64-wasi`   |   `wasm64-unknown-wasi`   |   as above, plus `-mwasm64`");
    table_row(24, "`wasm32-freestanding`   |   `wasm32-unknown-unknown`   |   `wasm-ld --no-entry --export=glo_main`");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\A WASI build synthesizes a `_start` wrapper around `main` and reaches
        \\the outside world through the `wasi_snapshot_preview1` import
        \\namespace. The module built from a program that prints one line
        \\exports `memory` and `_start` and imports `fd_write`.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\`wasm32-freestanding` is the other shape entirely. There is no WASI,
        \\no C library and no import namespace: the module built from the
        \\program below has no import section at all, and its export section
        \\holds exactly `memory` and `glo_main`. Everything the program needs is
        \\in the module, which is what makes the result loadable by a browser
        \\with a bare `WebAssembly.instantiate`.
    );
    code_block(27, "glosso", #string
        \\add :: (a: s32, b: s32) -> s32 #c_call { return a + b; }
        \\
        \\main :: (seed: s32) -> s32 #c_call {
        \\    if seed < 0 return add(1, 2);
        \\    return seed;
        \\}
    );
    code_block(28, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --target wasm32-freestanding -o wexp.wasm wexp.glo
        \\glosso: compile wexp.glo (target=wasm32-unknown-unknown)
        \\glosso: link wexp.wasm (target=wasm32-unknown-unknown, -O2)
        \\glosso: done wexp.wasm  (backend=llvm, target=wasm32-unknown-unknown, -O2)
        \\# module exports: memory, glo_main
    );
    paragraph(29, #string,oneline
        \\`main` takes a parameter and returns a value there, which no other
        \\target allows. On `wasm32-freestanding` a `#c_call main` is exempt from
        \\the rule that `main` takes no parameters, because nothing wraps it: the
        \\host calls the export directly and passes whatever the signature says.
        \\Elsewhere the same declaration is `error: main must not take
        \\parameters; use get_cmdline_args() for command-line arguments`.
    );
    paragraph(30, #string,oneline
        \\Anything beyond `glo_main` has to be exported explicitly, and has to be
        \\reachable. Reachability is computed before lowering, so an unreferenced
        \\`#c_call` function is pruned and the export then names a symbol that
        \\does not exist.
    );
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --target wasm32-freestanding \
        \\      --linker-arg "--export=glo_add" -o wexp3.wasm wexp.glo
        \\glosso: compile wexp.glo (target=wasm32-unknown-unknown)
        \\glosso: link wexp3.wasm (target=wasm32-unknown-unknown, -O2)
        \\glosso: done wexp3.wasm  (backend=llvm, target=wasm32-unknown-unknown, -O2)
        \\# module exports: memory, glo_add, glo_main
    );
    note(32, #string,oneline
        \\`--export=glo_main` is passed unconditionally for this target, so
        \\`--no-main` on a program that has no `main` breaks the link with
        \\`wasm-ld: error: symbol exported via --export not found: glo_main`. A
        \\`wasm32-freestanding` program keeps a `main`, `#c_call` or otherwise.
    );
    bullet(33, #string,oneline
        \\Sanitizers are rejected for wasm: `LLVM memory sanitizer is not
        \\supported for target 'wasm32-unknown-wasi'`.
    );
    bullet(34, #string,oneline
        \\`#library,dyn` is rejected during lowering, with the message
        \\`#library,dyn is not supported for target 'wasm32-unknown-unknown'`
        \\followed by the instruction it was lowering. There is no dynamic
        \\loader to call.
    );
    bullet(35, #string,oneline
        \\A `#foreign` call names a library file, and the wasm linker resolves
        \\that to `-l<name>`, so a host-provided function cannot be declared with
        \\`#foreign`. Imports come from undefined symbols admitted by
        \\`--linker-arg "--allow-undefined"`.
    );
    subheading(36, "How this manual is built");
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\The manual being read is a `wasm32-freestanding` module. A build script
        \\compiles the manual's entry file for that target, builds the Clay
        \\layout library from C sources for the same target, generates bindings
        \\for it, and links the result to a single `.wasm` file that the browser
        \\host loads.
    );
    code_block(38, "glosso", #string
        \\wasm_target := Output_Target.{
        \\    .os = Target_OS.Wasm,
        \\    .cpu = Target_CPU.Wasm32,
        \\    .abi = Target_ABI.Freestanding,
        \\};
        \\
        \\options: Compile_Options;
        \\set_target(*options, wasm_target);
        \\set_optimization_level(*options, .Optimized_Tiny);
        \\set_borrow_check(*options, Compiler_Borrow_Check.Error);
        \\set_temp_allocator_size(*options, 1024 * 1024);
        \\add_linker_arg(*options, "--export=glo_manual_set_view");
        \\add_linker_arg(*options, "--export=glo_manual_select_section");
        \\add_linker_arg(*options, "--allow-undefined");
        \\add_linker_arg(*options, "--initial-memory=134217728");
        \\built := compile("main.glo", "dist/manual.wasm", options);
    );
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\Every element of that configuration is forced by the target.
        \\`Optimized_Tiny` is `-Ozz`, chosen because the module is downloaded: a
        \\one-line program built for `wasm32-wasi` measures 84443 bytes at `-O1`
        \\and 23961 bytes at `-Ozz`. The explicit `--export=` arguments exist because only
        \\`glo_main` is exported by default. `--allow-undefined` admits the
        \\symbols the browser host supplies. And the manual's `main` is a
        \\`#c_call` procedure taking the viewport size and pointer state, which
        \\only this target permits.
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\The C dependency is built through the same target descriptor. The
        \\binding generator checks `OUT.os`, `OUT.cpu` and `OUT.abi` and returns
        \\a `#compile_error` string when they are not the wasm32 freestanding
        \\triple, so an accidental host build fails during checking instead of
        \\producing a module that cannot run. Generating Bindings with Bindgen
        \\and Build Scripts cover the two halves.
    );
}

render_section_140 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\Every source-level diagnostic is written to stderr in one layout,
        \\whatever phase produced it. The layout is a headline naming the level
        \\and the message, a location line, a framed excerpt of the offending
        \\source with a caret run under the span, and then zero or more notes.
    );
    code_block(1, "glosso", #string
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("hello from glosso\n");
        \\}
    );
    code_block(2, "text", #string
        \\error: unknown function print
        \\  --> …\diag1.glo:2:5
        \\  |
        \\2 |     print("hello from glosso\n");
        \\  |     ^^^^^
        \\help: did you mean 'tprint'?
    );
    table_header(3, "Line   —   Content");
    table_row(4, "headline   |   the level, a colon, and the message");
    table_row(5, "location   |   ` --> ` then the file path, line and column");
    table_row(6, "blank gutter   |   a bare `|` aligned with the line-number column");
    table_row(7, "source   |   the line number, ` | `, and the source line verbatim");
    table_row(8, "caret   |   a run of `^` under the span, one per character");
    table_row(9, "notes   |   a label, the note text, and optionally a frame of its own");
    paragraph(10, #string,oneline
        \\The gutter width is the width of the line number, so a diagnostic on
        \\line 4 indents the frame by one column less than one on line 16. The
        \\caret run is the span's length clamped to what remains on the line, and
        \\a span with no length still produces one caret. If the source line is
        \\empty or the line number is out of range the frame is omitted and only
        \\the headline is printed.
    );
    paragraph(11, #string,oneline
        \\Two note labels are produced: `help` for a suggested replacement, and
        \\`note` for everything else. A note may carry its own location frame,
        \\which is how a diagnostic points at a second place in the program.
    );
    code_block(12, "text", #string
        \\warning: resource must be released by 'array_free' before leaving scope
        \\  --> …\diag2.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
        \\note: release obligation was created here
        \\  --> …\diag2.glo:4:5
        \\  |
        \\4 |     values: [..]s64;
        \\  |     ^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(13, #string,oneline
        \\A note with no span prints as a bare label and message, with no frame
        \\under it. That form carries advice rather than a second location.
    );
    code_block(14, "text", #string
        \\error: tagged-union payload '.Ok' may only be bound by a pattern match
        \\  --> …\diag3.glo:7:20
        \\  |
        \\7 |     print("%\n", r.Ok);
        \\  |                    ^^
        \\note: match on the union and use that arm's payload capture, or enable #enable(allow_union_unwrap) for unsafe access
    );
    subheading(15, "Warnings and errors");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\Warnings and errors use the identical layout and differ in the headline
        \\word and its colour. An error ends the build and the compiler exits 1;
        \\a warning is printed and the build continues to completion with exit 0.
        \\Multiple diagnostics are separated by a blank line.
    );
    paragraph(17, #string,oneline
        \\When more than one error is rendered, a final summary line closes the
        \\group. Warnings never get a summary, however many there are.
    );
    code_block(18, "text", #string
        \\error: got f32, expected s32
        \\  --> …\diag8.glo:3:12
        \\  |
        \\3 |     return x;
        \\  |            ^
        \\
        \\error: got f64, expected s32
        \\  --> …\diag8.glo:8:12
        \\  |
        \\8 |     return y;
        \\  |            ^
        \\
        \\error: aborting due to 2 previous errors
    );
    paragraph(19, #string,oneline
        \\One family of warnings is configurable. `--borrow-check` takes `off`,
        \\`warning` or `error`, defaults to `warning`, and governs the temporal
        \\memory analysis: leaks, double releases, stale references and escaping
        \\pointers. `--borrow-check error` reprints exactly the same diagnostics
        \\with `error:` in the headline and stops the build. The `--borrow-check`
        \\Flag covers the analysis it controls.
    );
    note(20, #string,oneline
        \\`--borrow-check error` does not promote everything that looks like a
        \\warning. Checker lints, such as an unreachable pattern arm, are merged
        \\into the output after the promotion decision and stay warnings at every
        \\setting. `--borrow-check off` likewise silences the analysis but never
        \\a malformed `#memory` contract, which is always a hard error.
    );
    code_block(21, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --borrow-check error --backend llvm-ir -o d6.ll diag6.glo
        \\glosso: compile diag6.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\warning: unreachable pattern - already covered by an earlier case
        \\  --> …\diag6.glo:7:10
        \\  |
        \\7 |     case 1;   return "one again";
        \\  |          ^
        \\glosso: wrote d6.ll  (backend=llvm-ir, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
    );
    paragraph(22, #string,oneline
        \\Driver messages are a separate category and are not diagnostics. They
        \\are prefixed `glosso: `, carry no frame and no colour, and report
        \\command-line and toolchain problems rather than problems in the source.
        \\A bad option value exits 2; a build failure exits 1.
    );
    code_block(23, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --color rainbow diag1.glo
        \\glosso: --color must be auto, always, or never
        \\$ echo $?
        \\2
    );
    subheading(24, "Colour");
    paragraph(25, #string,oneline
        \\`--color` takes `auto`, `always` or `never` and defaults to `auto`.
        \\Under `auto`, colour is enabled only when stderr is a terminal and
        \\either the host is Windows or `TERM` is set to something other than
        \\`dumb`. Redirecting stderr to a file or a pipe therefore turns colour
        \\off without any flag.
    );
    table_header(26, "Setting   —   Effect");
    table_row(27, "`--color auto`   |   on when stderr is a terminal and the terminal is known to be capable");
    table_row(28, "`--color always`   |   on unconditionally");
    table_row(29, "`--color never`   |   off unconditionally");
    table_row(30, "`NO_COLOR` set to any value   |   off, and it beats `--color always`");
    code_block(31, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --color always diag1.glo
        \\^[[1;31merror^[[0m^[[1m: ^[[0m^[[1munknown function print^[[0m
        \\ ^[[1;34m --> ^[[0m…\diag1.glo:2:5
        \\
        \\$ NO_COLOR=1 glosso --color always diag1.glo
        \\error: unknown function print
        \\  --> …\diag1.glo:2:5
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\The escape byte is written `^[` in that transcript. Five escapes are
        \\used and nothing else: bold red for `error`, bold yellow for
        \\`warning`, bold blue for the arrow and the gutter, bold cyan for a
        \\note label and its frame, and plain bold for the message text. Because
        \\`NO_COLOR` is checked before the mode, a script can strip colour from
        \\a build it does not control.
    );
    subheading(33, "Diagnostics in generated source");
    paragraph(34, #string,oneline
        \\A string-valued `#insert` splices text produced at compile time into
        \\the program, and that text has no file of its own in the project. The
        \\renderer solves this by retargeting the diagnostic: the primary span is
        \\moved to the outermost `#insert` expression, which is a real place in a
        \\real file, and the position inside the generated text becomes a note.
    );
    code_block(35, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#insert emit_helper();
        \\
        \\emit_helper :: () -> string {
        \\    return "helper :: () -> s64 { return no_such_name; }\n";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", helper());
        \\}
    );
    code_block(36, "text", #string
        \\error: unbound 'no_such_name'
        \\  --> …\gen_bad.glo:3:1
        \\  |
        \\3 | #insert emit_helper();
        \\  | ^^^^^^^
        \\note: generated source failed here
        \\  --> …\cache\generated\insert\v1\82949d39….glo:1:30
        \\  |
        \\1 | helper :: () -> s64 { return no_such_name; }
        \\  |                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\Read that from the bottom up. The note names the line that is actually
        \\wrong and quotes it, and the path it names is a real file: the cache
        \\entry the generated text was written to, which can be opened in an
        \\editor. The headline names the `#insert` that produced it, which is the
        \\line to change.
    );
    paragraph(38, #string,oneline
        \\When one generated source inserts another, every link in the chain gets
        \\its own note labelled `generated by this #insert`, ordered from the
        \\failure outwards, and the headline lands on the `#insert` written by
        \\hand.
    );
    code_block(39, "text", #string
        \\error: unbound 'no_such_name'
        \\  --> …\gen_nest.glo:3:1
        \\  |
        \\3 | #insert outer();
        \\  | ^^^^^^^
        \\note: generated source failed here
        \\  --> …\insert\v1\82949d39….glo:1:30
        \\  |
        \\1 | helper :: () -> s64 { return no_such_name; }
        \\  |                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^
        \\note: generated by this #insert
        \\  --> …\insert\v1\e387a996….glo:1:1
        \\  |
        \\1 | #insert inner();
        \\  | ^^^^^^^
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\If the cache directory is unavailable the generated text is still
        \\rendered, but the note names the virtual path
        \\`<generated:#insert/<sha256>.glo>` instead, which cannot be opened.
        \\The Build Cache and Generated Sources covers where those files live and
        \\how to list them.
    );
}

render_section_141 :: () {
    paragraph(0, #string,oneline
        \\The compiler keeps one per-user cache root holding everything it can
        \\reuse between builds: materialized generated sources, finished
        \\artifacts, and static archives built from C sources for a target.
        \\`--print-cache-dir` resolves the root, prints it, and exits 0 without
        \\compiling anything.
    );
    code_block(1, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --print-cache-dir
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache
    );
    paragraph(2, #string,oneline
        \\The root is resolved once per process, from the first source in this
        \\list that yields an absolute path.
    );
    table_header(3, "Source   —   Value");
    table_row(4, "`--cache-dir <dir>`   |   the given directory, which must be absolute");
    table_row(5, "`GLOSSO_CACHE_DIR`   |   the given directory, which must be absolute");
    table_row(6, "Windows default   |   the known LocalAppData folder, then `glosso/cache`");
    table_row(7, "macOS default   |   `$HOME/Library/Caches/glosso`");
    table_row(8, "other POSIX default   |   `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/glosso`, else `$HOME/.cache/glosso`");
    paragraph(9, #string,oneline
        \\A relative path is rejected rather than resolved against the working
        \\directory, and the two spellings fail differently because one is a
        \\command-line error and the other is an environment error. `--cache-dir`
        \\prints `glosso: --cache-dir: cache directory override must be an
        \\absolute path` and exits 2; `GLOSSO_CACHE_DIR` prints `glosso: cache
        \\directory unavailable: GLOSSO_CACHE_DIR must contain an absolute path`
        \\and exits 1.
    );
    code_block(10, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --cache-dir "C:/temp/glosso-cache-probe" --print-cache-dir
        \\C:\temp\glosso-cache-probe
        \\$ GLOSSO_CACHE_DIR=C:/temp/env-cache glosso --print-cache-dir
        \\C:\temp\env-cache
        \\$ GLOSSO_CACHE_DIR=C:/temp/env-cache glosso --cache-dir C:/temp/flag-cache --print-cache-dir
        \\C:\temp\flag-cache
    );
    subheading(11, "The subtrees");
    table_header(12, "Path under the root   —   Contents");
    table_row(13, "`generated/insert/v1/<sha256>.glo`   |   materialized string-valued `#insert` sources");
    table_row(14, "`native/artifacts/v1/<key>.artifact` and `.manifest`   |   the final-artifact cache");
    table_row(15, "`native/v6/<name>/<target>/<key>/`   |   static archives built from C sources for a target");
    paragraph(16, #string,oneline
        \\The `v1` and `v6` components are format versions: a compiler that
        \\changes an entry's meaning changes the version and stops reading the
        \\old tree rather than misinterpreting it. `GLOSSO_NATIVE_CACHE_DIR`
        \\relocates the `native/v6` subtree alone, leaving the other two under
        \\the resolved root, so the C archives can be kept apart from the rest.
    );
    subheading(17, "The artifact cache");
    paragraph(18, #string,oneline
        \\The artifact cache stores the finished output of a build and restores
        \\it byte for byte when the same build is asked for again. A hit skips
        \\every phase, including checking, so it is reported by a single line and
        \\the build takes milliseconds.
    );
    code_block(19, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --run hello.glo
        \\glosso: compile hello.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: link hello.exe (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\glosso: done hello.exe  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\hello from 3
        \\
        \\$ glosso --run hello.glo
        \\glosso: restored hello.exe from the native artifact cache
        \\hello from 3
    );
    paragraph(20, #string,oneline
        \\Only three backends participate: `llvm`, `object` and `static-lib`.
        \\`shared-lib` and `llvm-ir` builds are never stored and never restored,
        \\and `--show-generated` disables the cache for the run it appears on.
        \\`GLOSSO_DISABLE_ARTIFACT_CACHE` set to any value disables it entirely.
    );
    paragraph(21, #string,oneline
        \\A cache entry is keyed by an identity string covering everything that
        \\could change the output.
    );
    bullet(22, #string,oneline
        \\The canonical path of the source file and the standard-library
        \\directory in use.
    );
    bullet(23, #string,oneline
        \\The LLVM triple, and separately every field of `OUT`, because the
        \\triple collapses distinct targets.
    );
    bullet(24, #string,oneline
        \\The backend name, the optimization level, the size mode, the sanitizer,
        \\the borrow-check mode, the temporary-allocator size, and the `--no-main`
        \\flag.
    );
    bullet(25, #string,oneline
        \\Every `--linker-arg` in command-line order, and every compile-time
        \\argument written after `--`.
    );
    paragraph(26, #string,oneline
        \\The `OUT` fields in that list are load-bearing. Compiling the same
        \\source for `x86_64-windows-msvc` and for `x86_64-freestanding-uefi`
        \\produces the same triple in the progress line, and the two builds still
        \\keep separate entries.
    );
    code_block(27, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --backend object --target x86_64-windows-msvc -o b.obj lib2.glo
        \\glosso: compile lib2.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: wrote b.obj  (backend=object, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O2)
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --backend object --target x86_64-windows-msvc -o b.obj lib2.glo
        \\glosso: restored b.obj from the native artifact cache
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --backend object --target x86_64-freestanding-uefi -o b.obj lib2.glo
        \\glosso: compile lib2.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: wrote b.obj  (backend=object, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O2)
        \\$ glosso -O2 --no-main --backend object --target x86_64-freestanding-uefi -o b.obj lib2.glo
        \\glosso: restored b.obj from the native artifact cache
    );
    paragraph(28, #string,oneline
        \\Identity decides which entry is consulted; a manifest decides whether
        \\that entry is still valid. The manifest records the length and
        \\modification time of every source file the build read, of the compiler
        \\executable itself, and of any `--linker-arg` value that names an
        \\existing file. Touching any one of them turns the next build back into
        \\a full compile.
    );
    note(29, #string,oneline
        \\A program with a top-level `#comptime` is never stored. Compile-time
        \\execution can write files, run commands or print, and restoring an
        \\artifact would skip all of it. Build scripts therefore always run,
        \\which is the behaviour a build script needs.
    );
    subheading(30, "Generated sources");
    paragraph(31, #string,oneline
        \\At top level, `#insert <expression>` evaluates the expression during
        \\checking and splices the resulting text into the program. That text is
        \\hashed with SHA-256, written to
        \\`generated/insert/v1/<hash>.glo` under the cache root, and the path of
        \\that file becomes the source name for every span inside the generated
        \\code. Publication is atomic, and an entry that already exists has its
        \\contents verified rather than rewritten.
    );
    paragraph(32, #string,oneline
        \\The name is the hash of the text alone, so the same generated source
        \\lands on the same file name under every cache root and in every
        \\project. That is what makes the entry shareable and makes a diagnostic
        \\pointing into it reproducible.
    );
    code_block(33, "glosso", #string
        \\#import "Io";
        \\
        \\#insert emit_helper();
        \\
        \\emit_helper :: () -> string {
        \\    return "helper :: () -> s64 { return 42; }\n";
        \\}
        \\
        \\main :: () {
        \\    print("%\n", helper());
        \\}
    );
    code_block(34, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --show-generated --run gen_ok.glo
        \\glosso: compile gen_ok.glo (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
        \\glosso: generated source C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\generated\insert\v1\74f6963b….glo
        \\glosso: link gen_ok.exe (target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\glosso: done gen_ok.exe  (backend=llvm, target=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, -O1)
        \\42
        \\
        \\$ cat C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\generated\insert\v1\74f6963b….glo
        \\helper :: () -> s64 { return 42; }
    );
    paragraph(35, #string,oneline
        \\`--show-generated` prints one `glosso: generated source <path>` line
        \\per materialized `#insert`, plus any cache warning as
        \\`glosso: warning: <text>`. It also disables the artifact cache for that
        \\run, since a restored artifact would report no generated sources at
        \\all.
    );
    paragraph(36, #string,oneline
        \\The lines are printed after checking succeeds and before lowering
        \\begins, so a build that fails inside generated code prints no such
        \\line. In that case the path arrives through the diagnostic instead:
        \\the note under the error names the same cache entry. Diagnostics:
        \\Reading and Configuring Them shows the rendered form.
    );
    paragraph(37, #string,oneline
        \\Redirecting the root moves the generated files with it, and the file
        \\name does not change, because it is derived from the text and not from
        \\the location.
    );
    code_block(38, "text", #string
        \\$ glosso --cache-dir C:/probe/mycache --show-generated --backend llvm-ir -o go.ll gen_ok.glo
        \\glosso: generated source C:\probe\mycache\generated\insert\v1\74f6963b….glo
    );
    paragraph(39, #string,oneline
        \\When no cache root can be resolved at all, the text is still compiled
        \\and the source name falls back to the virtual form
        \\`<generated:#insert/<hash>.glo>`. Nothing is written, so nothing can be
        \\opened, and the same build repeats the work every time.
    );
    paragraph(40, #string,oneline
        \\The third subtree is filled by the compile-time C archive builder.
        \\Importing a module that builds native code compiles its C sources once
        \\for the output target and caches the archive under
        \\`native/v6/<name>/<target>/<key>/`, which is why the first such import
        \\is slow and every later one is not. Embedding Lua and Generating
        \\Bindings with Bindgen cover the modules that use it.
    );
    code_block(41, "text", #string
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\native\v6\lua55
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\native\v6\glosso_rgfw
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\native\v6\glosso_wasm_compiler_rt_builtins
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\native\v6\glosso_wasm_compiler_rt_builtins\wasm32-wasm-freestanding
        \\C:\Users\almag\AppData\Local\glosso\cache\native\v6\glosso_wasm_compiler_rt_builtins\wasm32-wasm-wasi
    );
}
